--- body ---
## Context

Protocol_05 §3.4 (pre-registered revision (d)) bans Wikipedia / Wikidata /
DBpedia / mirrors / aggregator re-syndication as Source 1 or Source 2,
because a derivative of the reference dataset was published on Wikipedia
before the experiment. The protocol states: "Compliance is auditable:
Phase D synthesis counts Wikipedia/Wikidata citations in each output's
bibliography. Zero is compliant."

`slides/manuscript/main.md` (the arXiv preprint) discloses the
contamination risk but never answers the reader's next question: *did the
ban hold?* Author decision 2026-06-06: the compliance COUNT goes into
Annex C. It is a descriptive disclosure (a count, not an inferential
test), so it amends — not violates — ticket 0433's "no statistics in
Annex C" invariant. The full pre-registered H5 treatment (Wilson upper
bounds per agent) stays deferred to the report.tex chapter (ticket 0259).

## Scope

Three deliverables: script, macro, prose.

1. **Script** `src/aedist/audit_exp2_wiki_citations.py` (argparse,
   logging, no hardcoded paths — defaults from the same conventions as
   `src/aedist/extract_exp2_bib.py`, which already parses these files and
   whose helpers should be reused). Scan the 40 registered runs in
   `experiments/derived/arm1_flat/` (naive) and `arm2_flat/` (optimised):
   inventory Source 1 / Source 2 cells AND bibliography entries. Count
   citations whose URL/domain matches the banned set from protocol_05
   §3.4: `*.wikipedia.org` (any language subdomain, incl. `*.m.*`
   mirrors), `wikidata.org`, `dbpedia.org`, plus an explicit short list of
   known Wikipedia mirrors. Mechanical domain matching only; do NOT
   attempt LLM judgement of "aggregator re-syndication" — out of scope,
   note it as a limitation. Output: per-run CSV
   `report/inputs/generated/tab_exp2_wiki_compliance.csv`
   (agent, arm, run, n_cells_s1s2, n_banned_s1s2, n_bib_entries,
   n_banned_bib).

2. **Macro**: emit the aggregate values (total banned-citation count per
   arm, n runs scanned) as `\newcommand` macros for report/slides reuse.
   Per `.claude/rules/writing.md`: ≤3 new values → append to the existing
   generated macros file (e.g. `macros_slides.tex` generator), do NOT
   create a new `\InputIfFileExists` include (tectonic bundle trap).
   NOTE: `main.md` builds via pandoc and inputs no macro file — the prose
   quotes the literal number; guard it with an adherence/consistency test
   asserting the literal in main.md matches the generated CSV (pattern of
   ratchet ticket 0447).

3. **Prose**: one or two sentences in Annex C of
   `slides/manuscript/main.md` (l.256–339), adjacent to the existing
   leakage/contamination discussion. Report the count per arm. Phrasing
   discipline: if zero in the optimised arm, write "we did not detect any
   banned-domain citation across the N parsable optimised-arm outputs" —
   never "there are none" (protocol_05 reporting rule + writing.md
   epistemic humility). Naive-arm counts are NOT violations — the naive
   prompt (Doc-07) carries no source taxonomy; report them as unaided
   baseline behaviour, the contrast being itself informative.

## Makefile DAG

Wire the script into `experiments/render.mk` (P3 render workpackage) as
its own one-output-per-rule target; prerequisites must match the script's
actual reads (`arm1_flat`, `arm2_flat`) and writes. No P3-to-P3
side-output edges (ticket 0436 rule). Commit the generated CSV/macros
(handoff artifacts — never gitignored).

## First test

`tests/test_audit_exp2_wiki_citations.py`: feed a synthetic run markdown
containing (a) an inventory row citing `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X`
in Source 1, (b) a bibliography entry citing
`https://vi.m.wikipedia.org/...`, (c) a clean row citing `moit.gov.vn`.
Assert counts: n_banned_s1s2 == 1, n_banned_bib == 1, clean row not
counted. Plus the main.md literal-consistency adherence test.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Script counts banned-domain citations per run across the 40
      registered runs; CSV + macro artifacts generated and committed
- [ ] `experiments/render.mk` target added; DAG prerequisites match the
      script's actual reads/writes
- [ ] Annex C sentence(s) report the per-arm count with "we did not
      detect" phrasing for any zero; consistency test guards the quoted
      literal against the CSV
- [ ] Unit tests for the domain matcher; `make check` passes

